{
  "step_1": "The decision reason considers whether each lottery contains a sure outcome (i.e., an outcome with 100% probability). In this decision problem, neither Lottery A nor Lottery B contains a sure outcome. Lottery A yields -3000 Euros with 0.2% probability and 0 Euros with 99.8%, so no outcome occurs with certainty. Similarly, Lottery B yields -6000 Euros with 0.1% probability and 0 Euros with 99.9%, also without a sure outcome. Therefore, both lotteries lack a sure outcome. The decision reason prefers the lottery *without* a sure outcome. Since both lotteries meet this criterion, the reason would be indifferent between them. However, the reason is still applicable in principle because the absence of a sure outcome in both can be assessed from the problem.",
